# Keep the asset SOURCES out of the Vercel deployment.
#
# public/brand/ is a staging area, not a served directory: Canva exports at full
# camera resolution, each `.svg` really an 8-bit alpha mask plus a 3872x2581
# photograph in base64. scripts/extract-team.py turns them into public/team/*.webp,
# scripts/extract-decor-cutouts.py into public/decor/*.webp, and those processed
# files -- not these -- are what the site serves. Shipping the sources put 200MB
# on the CDN that no request ever asked for.
#
# They stay in the repo so the scripts still run from a fresh clone. Full-resolution
# copies are also archived outside it, at ~/Desktop/tka-v2-asset-sources.
#
# This is an explicit deny-list rather than an allow-list on purpose. A new file
# dropped into public/brand/ and referenced from somewhere will deploy; with an
# allow-list it would 404 in production and render as a blank slot, which is the
# far more expensive mistake.
#
# The files NOT listed here are served directly and must keep deploying. Two
# ways of deriving that list are wrong, and both were tried:
#
#   1. **Grepping only app/globals.css.** The eight gallery photos a.jpg-h.jpg
#      are not in any stylesheet -- they are hard-coded as `src: "/brand/a.jpg"`
#      inside components/academy-blog-framer/chunks/chunk-SX5HLRXN.js, a patched
#      Framer chunk. Excluding them shipped a /gallery with eight broken images
#      while every local check passed, because locally nothing is ignored.
#      Search the WHOLE tree, not the CSS.
#
#   2. **Grepping without stripping comments.** cloud.svg, perez.svg, peter.svg
#      and story.jpg appear only inside `/* ... */` in globals.css; ladybug.webm
#      and robo.jpg only in `//` comments; lady.webm and output.webm only as
#      script inputs in scripts/encode-decor.py. All eight are correctly
#      excluded -- a naive grep says otherwise and would re-add 24MB.
#
# So: a file deploys only if a live, non-comment `/brand/<name>` appears outside
# scripts/. Verify a change here against a real deployment, not a local build --
# .vercelignore has no effect on `next dev` or `next build`.

